WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:04.370
Imagine you’re an interstellar traveler.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:08.460
What sorts of alien life might you find out
there in the universe?

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:13.179
It’s interesting to speculate on how life
might evolve in the depths of a planet covered

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:19.010
by oceans, or the skies of a world with an
atmosphere teeming with floating life.

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.939
Perhaps one day, humanity will be able to
set out into the universe, and document these

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:26.630
unfamiliar worlds first-hand.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:32.410
This concept is explored in great scientific
detail in The Teeming Universe: an interstellar

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:36.440
field guide by speculative author and artist
Christian Cline.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.880
The book, which I have a link to down below,
explores how alien life might evolve on various

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.570
types of planets much different from our own.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.649
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
be joining a scientific vessel on a journey

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:54.430
into the beyond — and will explore just
some of the worlds that Cline covers in his

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.020
field guide.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.949
Several million light-years from Earth, the
first fictional planet we’re touching down

00:00:59.949 --> 00:01:02.290
on… doesn’t look like much.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:08.230
This is planet Menir, a world of ice and rock
with a diminished atmosphere and high levels

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.450
of ultraviolet radiation.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.480
Stepping out onto the surface, it seems like
there isn’t any life here.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.010
But that’s because to find life on Menir,
you have to look under a microscope.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.220
That’s right, the first alien life we’ve
found is a kind of bacteria.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:27.360
It might seem disappointing, and there be
will much larger aliens to come later in this

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.330
entry, but even discovering microscopic life
on an exoplanet would be a huge scientific

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.330
leap forward.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.080
And just because these lifeforms look like
Earth microorganisms to the untrained eye,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.080
doesn’t mean they’re the same.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.170
Called Menirophiles by the artist, he imagines
their unique purplish-pink color helps them

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:51.740
reflect excess radiation; because they have
hydrogen-peroxide within their bodies, instead

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.910
of water, they don’t freeze.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:58.140
While such a mixture would be deadly to carbon-based
Earth bacteria, menirophiles are protected

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.430
from the harsh effects by silicon in their
molecular structure.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:06.860
So, while the life on the desolate Menir may
be tiny, it’s exceptionally resilient.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:12.390
But the time has come to leave this exoplanet,
and head somewhere where life gets a little

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.850
bigger…

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:18.320
At first glance, our next planet — Ateria
— appears far more Earth-like.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.320
With a protective atmosphere and liquid oceans,
Ateria offers a much better opportunity to

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.980
find complex alien life.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.780
Touching down on the surface, it’s a beautiful
foggy day.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:34.600
Out of the mist rise grand plant like structures,
and the air is filled with the buzzing of

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:35.600
animals.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.930
Yet we’ll find Ateria is much different
than earth in one critical way…

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.130
its seasons.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:46.280
On earth, seasonal change happens gradually
due to earth’s axial tilt, with most animals

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:50.160
having numerous strategies to adapt to the
predictable changes.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.280
But due to a past asteroid impact, Ateria
has a wildly elliptical orbit: meaning it

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.240
moves close to its sun for some of its orbit,
and far out into the cold of space for the

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.240
rest.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.510
This pattern means the seasons on Ateria are
truly extreme… and life on this planet has

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.270
had to get creative.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:12.571
Beginning with plants, we might spot Strungs,
which possess a red tint to get the most out

00:03:12.571 --> 00:03:14.890
of the sun’s slightly dimmer light.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:20.170
During winters when the environment cools,
Strungs partially hollow bark retains insulating

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.800
air, allowing for extra heat protection.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.070
But other organisms — like the Fisher Crame,
the first animal we’ve encountered on our

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:31.080
voyage — have a different strategy to survive
the seasonal changes.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.650
They might look like insects on earth, but
Fisher Crames are far larger, with wingspans

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:38.630
of four feet, or 1.2 meters.

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:45.270
To outsmart the cold, these creatures simply
die after laying eggs, which a new generation

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:49.860
emerges from when temperatures rise again
to start the cycle anew.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:54.180
Another insect-like creature on Ateria, the
Tripperhog, has the more familiar strategy

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:59.099
of hibernating during the long winter, with
their thick shells camouflaging them and protecting

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:00.360
them from harm.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.280
Another clever solution.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:08.250
Ateria is a fascinating and extreme world,
but we must look to the horizon once again,

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.860
and see how much more incredible alien life
can get.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:16.220
The next stop on our journey is a massive
fictional planet called Toreya.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:21.260
The brilliant blue of the planet comes from
the fact that 99% of the surface is comprised

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.250
of an incredible planetwide ocean.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:28.780
Life in our own oceans can already be so alien,
so you can imagine what’s under the waves

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:30.590
of these endless waters…

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:36.230
The region closest to the surface is called
The Pelagic Zone — and contains entire ecosystems

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:39.880
founded on towering colonies of kelp-like
plants.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:47.240
Nicknamed ‘Heavenkelp,’ these plants can
grow to an astonishing 750 feet, or 228 meters

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.480
in length — almost like underwater trees.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:56.370
Among the leaves, aquatic life such as the
fish-like pelagonareans seek shelter and food.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:00.910
Some fish on Earth live in undersea forests
of kelp or seaweed for similar reasons.

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:05.600
Pelagonareans, or Pelegs for short, are a
highly diverse group that have evolved to

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.960
fill all manner of underwater niches.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:13.990
The incredible diversity of the Pelegs makes
the oceans of Toreya a teeming aquatic wilderness,

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:16.760
much like the richest areas of Earth’s oceans.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.620
The artist imagines that one species of Pelegs
— the gallant gulls — can even leave the

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.720
water altogether, as they’ve evolved powered
flight.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.380
Due to their aquatic ancestry, however, they
still need to occasionally dive back underwater

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:30.380
to breathe.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:34.180
But the Pelegs aren’t the only lifeforms
that lurk below the waves.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:39.580
Further down, in the twilight of the Bathypelagic
Zone, you can find various translucent or

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:45.000
bioluminescent species, feeding on floating
pieces of organic matter… or on each other.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.610
And it’s here where the largest organisms
on Toreya dwell: the Anoom — the most gigantic

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.230
creatures we’ve encountered on our journey
so far.

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:59.720
They’re able to reach a staggering 250 feet,
or 72 meters in length due to the vastness

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:04.780
of Toreya’s oceans, which the Anoom navigate
on an enormous transparent sail.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:09.640
Curiously, the Anoom are composed of vast
colonies of jellyfish-like invertebrates,

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:12.330
which work together as a single giant lifeform.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:17.580
And at the bottom of this planet-wide ocean,
you can find the Abyssopelagic zone: where

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.850
ghostly, pale-grey species swim through the
dark.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:25.580
In the absence of sunlight, many lifeforms
in the abyss survive off a chemical soup supplied

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:31.059
by hydrothermal vents, an extreme form of
survival that also occurs in our own oceans

00:06:31.059 --> 00:06:33.970
— proving life can take hold almost anywhere.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:38.780
And the next imaginary planet on our interstellar
journey is no exception.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:43.930
At 64% of earth’s mass, Yulene is the smallest
planet we’ve visited yet.

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.440
Its gravity is therefore so low it’s on
the very edge of the minimum for planetary

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.930
habitability — which has had a fascinating
impact on the planet’s native life.

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:58.130
Much of Yulene’s surface is locked in permanent
ice caps, as the low gravity has resulted

00:06:58.130 --> 00:07:01.250
in a thin atmosphere that retains little heat.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:05.919
While some forms of life venture into these
frozen wastes, most of the planet’s organisms

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:08.430
live on the warmer equatorial tundra.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:13.760
Here, for the first time in our interstellar
voyage, we can spot trees similar to those

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.050
on earth.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:18.930
Similar doesn’t mean the same, however,
and upon closer inspection of their leaves,

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.080
you can spot some key differences.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:25.820
Due to a higher presence of blue visible light
on Yulene, leaves have evolved to use not

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:29.860
chlorophyll, but the yellow pigment xanthophyll
for photosynthesis.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:34.570
Above the yellow foliage, you can spot the
magnificent Clerei, flying animals similar

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:36.520
to birds or pterosaurs.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:41.880
These low-gravity fliers have wingspans of
nearly 40 feet, or 12 meters — which they

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:47.120
use to soar on mountain thermals that help
them achieve lift off despite the thin atmosphere.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:51.789
After mating, Clerei build nests in the branches
of Yulene’s trees, raising their young in

00:07:51.789 --> 00:07:53.970
pairs like many species of birds.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:59.300
And baby Clerei are called Cerilets, which
is one of my favorite details.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.780
Clerei typically feed on animals like the
aquatic Cobbleback Trate, a creature that

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:08.680
gets its name from a pattern of stone-colored
markings that help camouflage it from predators

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:10.020
gliding overhead.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.630
To lay their eggs, the Cobbleback Trate, like
many freshwater Earth fish, must make a trying

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:20.090
voyage upstream to the frigid regions where
predators are scarce.One creature that doesn’t

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:25.130
have to worry about predators, however, is
the armored Sisobe, low-gravity grazers that

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:28.780
can grow up to 20 ft, or 6 meters in length.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:33.250
From the edge of the tundra to the continental
deserts, you can find these shaggy giants,

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:36.669
which move in great herds not unlike Earth
bison.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:41.899
And as the sun sets on Yulene, many plants
let off a brilliant glow to attract pollinating

00:08:41.899 --> 00:08:42.899
species.

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:47.250
It’s a dazzling display, but it’s time
to continue our voyage…

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:52.259
And the next imagined world is perhaps the
most extreme so far.

00:08:52.259 --> 00:08:57.649
Borold is a planet that closely orbits a red
dwarf star: only 20% the size of our sun,

00:08:57.649 --> 00:08:59.220
and far cooler.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:03.639
Borold is also tidally locked, with one side
of the planet perpetually facing the sun in

00:09:03.639 --> 00:09:07.350
endless day, and the other trapped in endless
night.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:11.220
Yet despite the harshness of Borold, there
is life upon this rock.

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.439
In the less extreme twilight region between
the light and dark sections of the planet,

00:09:15.439 --> 00:09:17.449
you can find the Borold Stars.

00:09:17.449 --> 00:09:22.610
The largest animals on this planet’s surface,
these creatures can grow up to 30 ft, or 9

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:23.760
meters long.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.480
Since the gravity on this planet is almost
3 times that of earth, Borolds creep slowly

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:32.730
across the ground — almost like giant terrestrial
starfish.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:36.470
The real challenge for Borold Stars, however,
is food and water.

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:42.110
Unable to hunt due to their top speed being
one mile, or 1.6 kilometers per hour, Borold

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:46.230
Stars simply crawl over anything edible, and
crush it under their body.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:51.629
And when they find water, they can drink hundreds
of gallons at a time.

00:09:51.629 --> 00:09:53.790
Borold Stars aren’t alone on this rock,
however.

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:57.649
There are also plants… just not plants as
we know them.

00:09:57.649 --> 00:10:03.779
These are Vleian Ojur, which look like jagged,
tall stones: but are actually cone-shaped

00:10:03.779 --> 00:10:07.689
photosynthetic lifeforms most analogous to
Earth trees.

00:10:07.689 --> 00:10:12.500
Their design helps protect them from the planet’s
brutal winds, which are the result of the

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:16.009
extreme temperature gradients produced by
the planet’s tidal lock.

00:10:16.009 --> 00:10:21.410
Our final Borold organism, however, can take
advantage of these perpetual winds to achieve

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:22.410
lift-off.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:26.231
You might not expect to find flying organisms
on a world with such crushing gravity, but

00:10:26.231 --> 00:10:30.970
Borold Kites are scavengers that let these
currents carry them to their next meal.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:31.970
Unique?

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:36.420
Certainly, but even more fantastical lifeforms
might be waiting on the final stop of our

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:37.750
journey.

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:42.190
The last fictional planet we’ll be visiting
is also the largest of them all.

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:48.040
A world somewhere between a terrestrial planet
and a gas giant, Hurcelion has a thick atmosphere

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:52.160
divided into layered ecosystems, almost like
an aerial ocean.

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:57.079
And it is in this atmosphere where we’ll
find some of the most awe-inspiring life in

00:10:57.079 --> 00:10:58.439
all the cosmos.

00:10:58.439 --> 00:11:03.680
In the stratosphere, balloon-like animals
float on sacs of internal hydrogen, steering

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:06.769
themselves through the air on specialized
fins.

00:11:06.769 --> 00:11:12.000
Many of these animals feed on greed clouds
of areofoliage, groups of small floating plants

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.120
held aloft by the wind, which contribute to
the high oxygen levels in Hurcelion’s atmosphere.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:22.999
And feeding on the herbivores are Speldos:
airborne predators which despite being twice

00:11:22.999 --> 00:11:26.029
as long as a school bus, are quite agile.

00:11:26.029 --> 00:11:30.950
Speldos lack jaws, but their gaping mouths
are filled with teeth, so to hunt they simply

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:32.720
ram into their prey.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:35.819
But the Speldos aren’t the true titans of
these skies.

00:11:35.819 --> 00:11:40.920
Oones are the largest animal we’ve seen
on this expedition, at over three times the

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:42.709
size of a blue whale.

00:11:42.709 --> 00:11:47.699
Indeed, Oones are more than just singular
lifeforms, and incorporate dozens of symbiotic

00:11:47.699 --> 00:11:49.429
species into their bodies.

00:11:49.429 --> 00:11:53.769
Oone mites are among the most important, as
they act as the colony’s guardians.

00:11:53.769 --> 00:11:58.389
While normally docile, they become fierce
protectors when the Oone they call their home

00:11:58.389 --> 00:11:59.389
is threatened.

00:11:59.389 --> 00:12:04.629
Sometimes, however, an Oone passes away and
falls through the atmosphere to Hurcelion’s

00:12:04.629 --> 00:12:07.989
surface, in a striking event known as an Oonefall.

00:12:07.989 --> 00:12:13.370
Below the calmer upper-atmosphere, the surface
of Hurcelion is a volcanic, stormy wasteland

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:16.529
— and one of the most extreme regions glimpsed
so far.

00:12:16.529 --> 00:12:22.130
Yet even here there is life, like the tiny
Praglerworms, which you can spot illuminating

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:25.410
the dark with their bioluminescent displays.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:31.060
These organisms prove, once and for all, that
life can take advantage of even the most extreme

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:32.749
scenarios in this Teeming Universe.

00:12:32.749 --> 00:12:37.089
Although our journey through the cosmos is
over for now, Christian Cline’s book The

00:12:37.089 --> 00:12:42.440
Teeming Universe contains many more incredible
speculative worlds and alien creatures, and

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:45.149
I encourage you to check out the link to it
below.

00:12:45.149 --> 00:12:50.459
I’ve also included a link in the description
to Astrovitae, a relatively new speculative

00:12:50.459 --> 00:12:53.889
biology magazine you can read online for free.

00:12:53.889 --> 00:12:57.720
The Teeming Universe was recently featured
there, and they’ve got a lot of other cool

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:01.639
stuff too, so I wanted to spread the word
to fans of the genre.

00:13:01.639 --> 00:13:03.809
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:13:03.809 --> 00:13:08.570
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:11.660
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:16.869
See you in the next video.

